  "method": "Almost everything Japanese food tastes of starts here, and the entire technique is a lesson in restraint: two ingredients, no browning, no salt, no boiling, and it is finished in under fifteen minutes. Understand it once and half of Japanese cooking opens up. Start with the kombu, and start it cold. Do not scrub the fine white bloom off the surface — that powder is mannitol and glutamate drying out of the seaweed itself, which is to say it is the flavour you are paying for, and every recipe that tells you to wipe the kombu clean is telling you to wash off the point. If there is visible grit or sand, brush it off dry with a cloth, and leave the white alone. Put the kombu in the cold water and give it time. Thirty minutes is good, a few hours in the fridge is better, and overnight is better still — a cold soak extracts the glutamate slowly and cleanly and asks nothing of you. Now heat it, and heat it gently, and here is the one rule of dashi: THE KOMBU MUST NEVER BOIL. Bring the pan up slowly over a low flame, aiming to take a good ten minutes to reach the point where small bubbles are beginning to gather on the bottom of the pan and the water is steaming but not moving — around 60 to 65°C, which in practical terms is the moment before it looks like anything is happening. Pull the kombu out at that point with tongs. Past roughly 80°C the seaweed starts giving up alginates instead of glutamate, and the stock turns slippery, faintly bitter and unmistakably marine in a way it should not be. That change is not reversible, and a boiled dashi cannot be corrected by any later step. Once the kombu is out, bring the water to a proper boil for a few seconds, then kill the heat completely and let it settle for thirty seconds. Now add all the katsuobushi at once and DO NOT STIR IT. Stirring beats the flakes apart, clouds the stock and pushes bitterness out of them; left alone they will swell, sink and release what they have in about a minute. Give them sixty to ninety seconds, no more, then strain the whole thing through a fine sieve lined with a piece of muslin or a clean cloth. And then the second rule, which matters as much as the first: DO NOT SQUEEZE THE FLAKES. Let it drip, and let it take a minute or two. Squeezing the sodden bonito is exactly the same mistake as boiling the kombu and produces the same result — a cloudy stock with a bitter, slightly fishy edge — and it is the most common way a home cook ruins a dashi they otherwise made perfectly. What you should have is a clear, pale golden liquid that smells of the sea and of woodsmoke and tastes savoury, round and almost sweet, with no salt in it at all. Taste it on its own before you use it, because you need to know what it tastes like unseasoned in order to recognise it later underneath everything else. Do not throw the spent kombu and katsuobushi away. They still hold a great deal, and the second extraction is a real ingredient in its own right — see the leftovers below.",
