  "chefNotes": "Kombu into cold water and never let it boil — pull it at 60–65°C. Katsuobushi off the heat, no stirring, 60–90 seconds. Strain and never squeeze. If your dashi is cloudy or bitter, one of those three is why.",
  "pairsWith": "It is the base rather than the partner — miso soup, chawanmushi, every simmered vegetable, the batter of an okonomiyaki and the broth of a donburi. Keep a jar in the fridge and you will find uses for it that have nothing to do with Japan.",
  "trivia": "Umami was discovered in this exact bowl. In 1908 Kikunae Ikeda, a chemistry professor at Tokyo Imperial University, became preoccupied with a question about his wife's dashi: it tasted strongly of something that was clearly not sweet, sour, salty or bitter, and the four-taste model of the day had no room for it. He boiled down a very large quantity of kombu, crystallised out the compound responsible, identified it as glutamic acid, and named the taste umami — savoury. He patented the manufacture of its sodium salt the following year, which is how monosodium glutamate came to exist. The better half of the story arrived five years later: in 1913 his student Shintaro Kodama went looking for the same thing in katsuobushi and found something different, inosinate, the savoury compound of dried fish and meat. Glutamate and inosinate are synergistic, and not mildly so — put together they register as several times more savoury than the sum of the two alone. So the two-ingredient stock that Japanese cooks had been making for centuries turns out to be a precisely built chemical amplifier, and it took Western analytical chemistry until the twentieth century to explain what the technique had already worked out. The katsuobushi deserves a word of its own, because it may be the most laboriously made ingredient in any kitchen: bonito is filleted, simmered, boned, smoked over weeks, then deliberately inoculated with a mould, sun-dried, re-inoculated, and repeated over months until the fillet is dry, dense and hard enough to be shaved on what is essentially a woodworking plane. It is routinely described as the hardest food in the world, and that is not a figure of speech.",
