  "ingredients": "300g chicken wings and carcass, chopped · 60g pork belly, in one piece, for the chashu · 1.2L cold water · 8g dried kombu · 6g katsuobushi (dried bonito flakes) · 25g onion, halved · 15g fresh ginger, sliced · 10g garlic, crushed · 3 spring onions · 45ml light soy sauce (for the tare) · 15ml mirin · 15ml sake · 8g white sugar · 120g fresh ramen noodles · 1 egg · 20ml neutral oil (for the aroma oil) · 15g spring onion greens (for the aroma oil) · 20g menma (seasoned bamboo shoots), optional · 1/4 sheet nori",
  "method": "The single most useful thing to understand about ramen is that a bowl is not one recipe but THREE separate things made separately and assembled at the last second: the soup, the tare, and the aroma oil. And the second most useful thing follows from it, and it is the thing almost every home cook gets wrong: THE BOWL IS SEASONED BY THE TARE, NOT BY THE SOUP. The broth itself is left essentially unsalted. If you taste your finished ramen broth on its own and it tastes properly seasoned and delicious, you have made a soup, and when the tare and the noodles go in you will get something too salty and flat. It should taste rich, savoury and slightly underwhelming on its own. That is correct. Start the soup. Cover the chopped chicken bones with cold water in a pot, bring it to the boil, and let it boil hard for two or three minutes, at which point a great deal of grey scum will rise. Tip the whole lot into the sink, then wash each piece under the tap and SCRUB the bones with your fingers, and wash the pot as well. This is fifteen minutes of unglamorous work and it is what separates a clear golden broth from a murky beige one. Return the bones to the clean pot with fresh cold water, the onion, ginger, garlic and the white parts of the spring onion, bring it barely to the boil and then drop it immediately to the faintest possible movement — an occasional bubble, nothing more. Three hours at that whisper. Add the kombu for the last thirty minutes only and never let it boil, and the katsuobushi for the last two minutes off the heat, exactly as in the dashi. Strain. Meanwhile, roll the pork belly tightly and tie it, brown it all over in a dry pan, then simmer it very gently for two hours in a small pan of water with a splash of soy, mirin and sake until a skewer meets no resistance. Cool it in its liquid and chill it before slicing; a warm belly cannot be cut thin and thin is the whole point of chashu. Now the tare, which takes four minutes and does most of the work. Warm the soy, mirin, sake and sugar together with a small piece of kombu until the sugar dissolves, and do not boil it — you want the mirin and sake to lose their raw alcohol edge, not to reduce. Take it off, let the kombu sit in it while it cools, and remove it. That is roughly enough tare for two or three bowls, and it will keep in the fridge for weeks. The aroma oil is faster still: warm the neutral oil with the sliced spring onion greens over a low heat for six or seven minutes until the greens are frizzled and the oil smells strongly of them, then strain. Do it while something else is simmering. The egg wants six and a half minutes from the fridge into already-boiling water, then straight into iced water, then peeled and left in a splash of the tare thinned with water for four hours or overnight. Do not leave it in the marinade longer than about twelve hours; past that the white turns rubbery and unpleasantly salty, and there is no way back from it. Now assemble, and assemble fast, because this is where a good bowl is won or lost. Fill the serving bowl with boiling water to heat it through, and heat the soup to a genuine rolling boil at the same time. Put a big pot of UNSALTED water on for the noodles — unsalted because everything about this bowl has been measured, and salted noodle water quietly adds an amount of salt nobody accounted for. Tip out the hot water from the bowl. Into the empty hot bowl goes 30ml of tare and a teaspoon of the aroma oil. Pour about 300ml of boiling soup in on top of them and swirl it once; the pouring does the mixing. Cook the noodles in the meantime — fresh ramen noodles want 60 to 90 seconds and no more — then lift them out and SHAKE THEM BONE DRY, hard, several times. Water clinging to noodles is the last enemy: it dilutes a tare you measured to the millilitre and turns a sharp bowl into a vague one. Fold the noodles neatly into the soup, top with sliced chashu, the halved egg, menma, sliced spring onion and the nori tucked against the rim, and take it to the table immediately. From the moment the noodles hit the water you have about three minutes before the bowl starts declining, which is why every ramen shop in Japan is built around a counter.",
