  "chefNotes": "Three components, not one recipe. The tare seasons the bowl, so the broth stays almost unsalted. Blanch and scrub the bones. Unsalted noodle water, and shake the noodles bone dry. Assemble in twenty seconds and eat immediately.",
  "pairsWith": "Gyoza on the side is the standard pairing and needs no defending. A cold beer, a small dish of something pickled and sharp, and nothing else \u2014 ramen is not a dish that wants company.",
  "trivia": "Japan's most famous food is Chinese, and Japan said so in the name for the first half of the twentieth century: until the 1950s it was generally called shina soba or chuka soba, both of which simply mean Chinese noodles. It arrived with Chinese cooks in the treaty ports after 1859, and Yokohama's Chinatown is the usual starting point. What turned a port-city curiosity into the national dish, though, was neither Chinese nor Japanese but American. Japan's rice harvest failed catastrophically in 1945 and the country was starving; the occupation authorities shipped in enormous quantities of surplus American wheat flour as food aid, and the Japanese government, holding a mountain of wheat and almost no rice, actively promoted bread and noodles in schools and public campaigns. Cheap flour plus a hungry population plus a black market full of small stalls is the entire recipe for ramen's explosion, and nearly every regional style that people now discuss as though it were ancient tradition was invented in the decades immediately after: Sapporo's miso ramen in the 1950s, Hakata's tonkotsu around the same time, the instant packet in 1958. The dish is younger than most of the people who tell you how it should be made, which is worth remembering when someone gets very serious about the rules.",
