      "howThisFeels": "A tower rather than a pancake \u2014 crisp noodles, a mountain of sweet steamed cabbage and a soft fried egg holding the whole thing down.",
      "trivia": "Hiroshima's version is not a variation on the Osaka one so much as a rebuttal of it. Nothing is mixed: a paper-thin crepe goes down first, then an enormous heap of cabbage on top of it, then the pork, and the whole tower is flipped as a unit and left to steam under its own weight for several minutes while a nest of noodles fries alongside and an egg cooks flat on the griddle. It is then assembled in reverse. The engineering reason for the layers is that the cabbage steams rather than fries, so you can use far more of it \u2014 roughly double \u2014 without it going wet, and the sweetness of a large volume of gently steamed cabbage is the whole character of the dish. It is also considerably harder to do at home than the Osaka style, and the flip is the moment it lives or dies, which is precisely why Hiroshima cooks are proud of it.",
